/*******************************************************************************
* McXtrace instrument: battery_xray
*
* %Identification
* Written by: Claude (Cowork), for Peter Willendrup
* Origin: McXtrace / Union framework demonstration
* %INSTRUMENT_SITE: Templates
*
* An approximate McXtrace/Union model of a cylindrical (18650-type) Li-ion
* battery cell "jellyroll" for X-ray simulation, built with the xraylib-
* based Union processes (Compton_xrl_process, Rayleigh_xrl_process) instead
* of NCrystal - the X-ray sibling of LiIon_Battery_Union.instr (the
* neutron/NCrystal version of the same cell, written for McStas).
*
* %Description
* Same "onion model" geometry as the neutron version: the wound jellyroll
* is approximated as five concentric Union_cylinder shells (steel can,
* cathode composite, separator+electrolyte, anode composite, central
* mandrel), nested by giving smaller-radius shells higher `priority` so
* each one punches its own annulus out of the shell outside it.
*
* The physics is genuinely different from the neutron model, which is the
* interesting part of this exercise:
*
* - McXtrace's xraylib Union processes (Compton_xrl_process,
*   Rayleigh_xrl_process, KN_xrl_process) are single-ELEMENT processes
*   (density + atomic number), not compound "cfg string" materials like
*   NCrystal. A compound is therefore built the "long way": one
*   Compton_xrl_process + Rayleigh_xrl_process pair per element, each
*   given a `density` equal to (bulk compound density) x (mass fraction
*   of that element) x (volume fraction of that phase in the shell),
*   then all combined into one Union_make_material via a comma-separated
*   process_string. This is the standard mixture rule for X-ray mass
*   attenuation coefficients, (mu/rho)_compound = sum_i w_i (mu/rho)_i,
*   applied per-shell exactly as the neutron file mixed NCrystal cfgs by
*   volume fraction.
* - Because elements are addressed directly, the cathode active material
*   no longer needs an oxide "proxy": LiCoO2 (a real, common cathode
*   chemistry, simpler to enumerate than NMC) is built exactly from its
*   Li/Co/O stoichiometry, rather than standing in for it.
* - The neutron/X-ray contrast mechanisms are different, which is worth
*   seeing explicitly: in the neutron model, the cathode shell's
*   absorption was completely dominated by natural lithium (huge Li-6
*   capture cross section). Here, Li (Z=3) is essentially invisible to
*   X-rays, and the cathode shell's absorption is instead completely
*   dominated by cobalt (Z=27) - illustrating exactly the complementary-
*   contrast point the source paper (Ziesche et al., Joule 6, 35-52,
*   2022) makes about combining neutron and X-ray imaging of the same
*   cell. The steel can is also comparatively much more strongly
*   absorbing to hard X-rays than it is to thermal neutrons, consistent
*   with that paper's note that cylindrical steel-canned cells usually
*   need higher-energy/synchrotron X-rays (rather than a lab tube) to be
*   imaged at all - see the energy caveat below.
*
* Geometric approximation [assumed] - unchanged from the neutron model
* -----------------------------------------------------------------------
* Illustrative 18650-type dimensions (not a teardown of a specific cell):
* overall can outer radius 9.0 mm, wall 0.2 mm, active winding height
* 60 mm. Concentric shells, outside in:
*
*   r  8.8 - 9.0 mm : steel outer can                    -> Fe (elemental proxy)
*   r  4.6 - 8.8 mm : cathode composite (85/15 vol%)      -> LiCoO2 + Al
*   r  4.0 - 4.6 mm : separator + liquid electrolyte      -> polyethylene proxy
*   r  1.5 - 4.0 mm : anode composite (90/10 vol%)        -> graphite + Cu
*   r  0.0 - 1.5 mm : central winding mandrel/core hole   -> Vacuum
*
* Not modeled: end caps/vent/tabs/headspace, the true helical winding, and
* (as in the neutron file) pouch/prismatic stacked-electrode cells, which
* would need Union_box volumes instead.
*
* Beam-energy / my_absorption caveat [important, X-ray-specific]
* -----------------------------------------------------------------------
* Compton_xrl_process and Rayleigh_xrl_process compute their own
* (energy-dependent) scattering cross section from xraylib at run time -
* that part is exact for whatever beam energy the host instrument uses.
* Photoelectric absorption, however, is NOT computed by either of those
* processes (their `_physics_my` functions only return CS_Compt/CS_Rayl),
* so - exactly as with the neutron model's my_absorption standing in for
* NCrystal's (n,gamma) capture - it has to be supplied manually to
* Union_make_material via `my_absorption` [1/m]. Unlike thermal-neutron
* capture (which is a mild 1/v energy dependence away from resonances),
* X-ray photoelectric attenuation scales roughly as E^-3, so a fixed
* my_absorption is only valid near the ONE energy it was computed for.
* The values below were hand-estimated (order-of-magnitude, from memory
* of typical XCOM-style photoelectric mass-attenuation coefficients) for
* a representative hard-X-ray/synchrotron energy of 60 keV, in line with
* the source paper's point that a steel 18650 can typically needs such an
* energy (or higher) to be imaged in transmission at all. If you run this
* at a different energy, or care about absolute attenuation quantitatively,
* replace them with a proper lookup - xraylib is already a hard dependency
* of these components, so from Python:
*     import xraylib
*     xraylib.CS_Photo(Z, E_keV)   # photoelectric mass atten. coeff, cm^2/g
* then my_absorption = density[g/cm^3] * CS_Photo(Z,E) * 100  [1/m], summed
* over the elements in a shell exactly as done below. The two reference
* Union+xraylib examples shipped with McXtrace (PowderCompton_union.instr,
* Test_KN_Comp_Rayl_union.instr) simply set my_absorption=0 throughout
* (i.e. they ignore photoelectric absorption entirely) - that is a valid,
* simpler starting point too, but for a cell this steel- and cobalt-heavy
* it will visibly under-attenuate the beam relative to reality.
*
* Also available in McXtrace's Union/xraylib set but NOT used here, to
* keep this file's structure a direct parallel to the neutron version
* (one dominant scattering-physics choice per material, plus the manual
* absorption term): KN_xrl_process (free-electron Klein-Nishina Compton,
* density-only, ignoring atomic binding - a cruder alternative to
* Compton_xrl_process) and Powder_process (Bragg diffraction from a
* reflection list, for a genuinely crystalline sample - would need a
* verified .lau/.laz-type reflection file per material, not attempted
* here).
*
* For full compatibility with e.g. mcstas-antlr, this is a complete,
* valid DEFINE INSTRUMENT/TRACE/END file (matching the structure Peter
* converted the neutron fragment to), with the sample position exposed
* as a REMOVABLE COMPONENT Arm so it can still be trimmed out and the
* Union block reused elsewhere if desired.
*
* %Parameters
* (none)
*
* %Link
* Ziesche, R.F. et al., "Neutron imaging of lithium batteries", Joule 6,
* 35-52 (2022), https://doi.org/10.1016/j.joule.2021.12.003
* %Link
* McXtrace Union/xraylib examples: Tests_union/PowderCompton_union.instr,
* Tests_union/Test_KN_Comp_Rayl_union.instr (shipped with McXtrace)
* %End
*******************************************************************************/
DEFINE INSTRUMENT battery_xray()

TRACE

REMOVABLE COMPONENT sample_pos = Arm()
  AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

COMPONENT init = Union_init()
AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Materials. density=... below is always the EFFECTIVE density of that
 * one element's contribution within its shell (bulk density of the
 * element or compound x mass fraction in the compound, if any, x volume
 * fraction of that phase in the shell) - see header for the mixing rule.
 * my_absorption [1/m] on each Union_make_material is the sum of the
 * (photoelectric-only, 60 keV, [assumed]) contributions of its
 * constituent elements, computed the same way: density_eff * CS_Photo *100.
 * --------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* Steel can, proxied by elemental iron (Z=26). [assumed proxy: real
 * cans are low-carbon/stainless steel alloys, not pure Fe] */
COMPONENT steel_can_compt = Compton_xrl_process(density = 7.87, atomno = 26)
AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

COMPONENT steel_can_rayl = Rayleigh_xrl_process(density = 7.87, atomno = 26)
AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

COMPONENT steel_can_material = Union_make_material(
    process_string = "steel_can_compt,steel_can_rayl",
    my_absorption = 141.7 /* [assumed, hand-estimated at 60 keV, see header] */
)
AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

/* Anode composite: graphite active material (Z=6, 90 vol%, [assumed]) +
 * Cu current-collector foil (Z=29, 10 vol%, [assumed]), homogenized. */
COMPONENT anode_C_compt = Compton_xrl_process(density = 2.23*0.90, atomno = 6)
AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

COMPONENT anode_C_rayl = Rayleigh_xrl_process(density = 2.23*0.90, atomno = 6)
AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

COMPONENT anode_Cu_compt = Compton_xrl_process(density = 8.96*0.10, atomno = 29)
AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

COMPONENT anode_Cu_rayl = Rayleigh_xrl_process(density = 8.96*0.10, atomno = 29)
AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

COMPONENT anode_composite_material = Union_make_material(
    process_string = "anode_C_compt,anode_C_rayl,anode_Cu_compt,anode_Cu_rayl",
    my_absorption = 20.0 /* [assumed] = 2.007*0.0015*100 (C) + 0.896*0.22*100 (Cu), 1/m */
)
AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

/* Separator + liquid electrolyte, proxied by polyethylene (C: Z=6,
 * H: Z=1). Real separators are PE/PP; the liquid carbonate-solvent
 * electrolyte has no simple single-compound treatment here either, so
 * its H content is folded into this proxy too - see header. */
COMPONENT separator_C_compt = Compton_xrl_process(density = 0.94*0.8560, atomno = 6)
AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

COMPONENT separator_C_rayl = Rayleigh_xrl_process(density = 0.94*0.8560, atomno = 6)
AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

COMPONENT separator_H_compt = Compton_xrl_process(density = 0.94*0.1437, atomno = 1)
AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

COMPONENT separator_H_rayl = Rayleigh_xrl_process(density = 0.94*0.1437, atomno = 1)
AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

COMPONENT separator_material = Union_make_material(
    process_string = "separator_C_compt,separator_C_rayl,separator_H_compt,separator_H_rayl",
    my_absorption = 0.12 /* [assumed, hand-estimated at 60 keV, see header]; H's
                             contribution is negligible at X-ray energies, unlike
                             its dominant role in the neutron model */
)
AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

/* Cathode composite: real LiCoO2 stoichiometry (Li: Z=3, Co: Z=27,
 * O: Z=8; mass fractions 0.0709/0.6021/0.3270, bulk density 5.05 g/cm^3
 * [assumed typical value]) at 85 vol% [assumed], + Al current-collector
 * foil (Z=13) at 15 vol% [assumed]. Unlike the neutron model, no oxide
 * "proxy" is needed - xraylib works per element, so the real compound
 * can be entered directly. */
COMPONENT cathode_Li_compt = Compton_xrl_process(density = 5.05*0.0709*0.85, atomno = 3)
AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

COMPONENT cathode_Li_rayl = Rayleigh_xrl_process(density = 5.05*0.0709*0.85, atomno = 3)
AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

COMPONENT cathode_Co_compt = Compton_xrl_process(density = 5.05*0.6021*0.85, atomno = 27)
AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

COMPONENT cathode_Co_rayl = Rayleigh_xrl_process(density = 5.05*0.6021*0.85, atomno = 27)
AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

COMPONENT cathode_O_compt = Compton_xrl_process(density = 5.05*0.3270*0.85, atomno = 8)
AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

COMPONENT cathode_O_rayl = Rayleigh_xrl_process(density = 5.05*0.3270*0.85, atomno = 8)
AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

COMPONENT cathode_Al_compt = Compton_xrl_process(density = 2.70*0.15, atomno = 13)
AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

COMPONENT cathode_Al_rayl = Rayleigh_xrl_process(density = 2.70*0.15, atomno = 13)
AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

COMPONENT cathode_composite_material = Union_make_material(
    process_string = "cathode_Li_compt,cathode_Li_rayl,cathode_Co_compt,cathode_Co_rayl,cathode_O_compt,cathode_O_rayl,cathode_Al_compt,cathode_Al_rayl",
    my_absorption = 52.8 /* [assumed] = 0.006 (Li) + 51.67 (Co) + 0.49 (O) + 0.61 (Al), 1/m;
                             completely dominated by cobalt - the X-ray
                             mirror image of the neutron model, where this
                             same shell's absorption was completely
                             dominated by lithium instead */
)
AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Geometry: five concentric cylindrical shells ("onion model" of the
 * wound jellyroll), built from largest radius/lowest priority to
 * smallest radius/highest priority so each inner cylinder cuts its own
 * annulus out of the ones further out. All lengths in metres.
 * The whole stack is centred wherever the %include lands (RELATIVE
 * PREVIOUS), with the cylinder axis along local y (vertical), matching
 * how these cells are normally stood up for X-ray radiography/CT.
 * --------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* Outer steel can, radius 9.0 mm, wall thickness 0.2 mm [assumed,
 * illustrative 18650-scale dimensions]. */
COMPONENT battery_can = Union_cylinder(
    radius = 0.0090, yheight = 0.0600,
    priority = 1,
    material_string = "steel_can_material"
)
AT (0,0,0) RELATIVE sample_pos

/* Cathode composite fills the can interior out to the can wall.
 * yheight reduced by 0.2 mm from battery_can - see FIX note above. */
COMPONENT battery_cathode = Union_cylinder(
    radius = 0.0088, yheight = 0.0598,
    priority = 2,
    material_string = "cathode_composite_material"
)
AT (0,0,0) RELATIVE battery_can

/* Separator + electrolyte band. yheight reduced by another 0.2 mm. */
COMPONENT battery_separator = Union_cylinder(
    radius = 0.0046, yheight = 0.0596,
    priority = 3,
    material_string = "separator_material"
)
AT (0,0,0) RELATIVE battery_can

/* Anode composite band. yheight reduced by another 0.2 mm. */
COMPONENT battery_anode = Union_cylinder(
    radius = 0.0040, yheight = 0.0594,
    priority = 4,
    material_string = "anode_composite_material"
)
AT (0,0,0) RELATIVE battery_can

/* Central winding mandrel/core, modeled as an empty (Vacuum) hole -
 * some real cells instead have a solid plastic centre pin, which could
 * be represented by swapping "Vacuum" for a Polyethylene_CH2.ncmat-based
 * material analogous to separator_material above. yheight reduced by a
 * final 0.2 mm  */
COMPONENT battery_mandrel = Union_cylinder(
    radius = 0.0015, yheight = 0.0592,
    priority = 5,
    material_string = "Vacuum"
)
AT (0,0,0) RELATIVE battery_can

/* Single Union_master simulates all five geometries defined above in one
 * scattering step at the cell's position. */
COMPONENT battery_master = Union_master()
AT (0,0,0) RELATIVE battery_can

COMPONENT battery_stop = Union_stop()
AT (0,0,0) ABSOLUTE

END
